1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Most weak player-prop research starts and
ends with one screenshot:

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:06,120
the player’s last five games.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,730
But a green hit-rate chart does not tell
you whether tonight’s

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,640
line is good. The player’s role may have
changed.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,480
A teammate may be returning.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,280
The matchup may affect this stat
differently.

7
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:20,460
And the sportsbook may have already
adjusted the number.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,320
A better process checks six signals before
making any decision.

9
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,230
In this video, I’ll show you what those
signals are,

10
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,840
where to find them, and how Propeller
Picks brings them together.

11
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,320
Before looking at trends, define the
question precisely:

12
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:39,140
which player, which stat, which direction,
and which line?

13
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:42,950
For example, “Will this player go over
twenty-four and a half

14
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,420
points?” is a useful question.

15
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,480
“Is this player good tonight?” is not.

16
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:51,200
That half point matters.

17
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:54,120
So does the difference between points,
assists,

18
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:55,780
and a combined stat.

19
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,620
Always begin with the exact number
available to you,

20
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,020
because every other signal must be
evaluated against that line.

21
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:07,930
Signal one is volume: how many chances
will the player have

22
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:09,140
to produce the stat?

23
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:12,320
In basketball, that can mean expected
minutes,

24
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:14,080
usage, or potential assists.

25
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,340
In football, it may be routes, targets,

26
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:23,140
carries, or dropbacks. In baseball, think
plate appearances or batters faced.

27
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,460
In hockey, look at ice time, line
assignment,

28
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:31,780
and power-play role. Volume usually
creates the foundation for a projection.

29
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:35,350
A player can be talented and still fall
short if the

30
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:36,700
opportunity is not there.

31
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:40,020
Before asking how efficiently the player
has performed,

32
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,120
ask whether tonight’s role provides enough
chances to clear the line.

33
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:47,020
Signal two is matchup quality.

34
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:51,090
The key is to evaluate the matchup for
this specific player

35
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:52,960
and this specific stat.

36
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:56,780
An opponent can defend scoring well but
still allow assists.

37
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,650
A defense can limit rushing production
while giving up receiving work

38
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:05,160
to running backs. A baseball matchup can
change with pitcher handedness,

39
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,600
pitch mix, and the hitter’s expected place
in the order.

40
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:12,320
So avoid reducing the matchup to one
overall ranking.

41
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,730
Ask how the opponent affects the exact
path this player needs

42
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:18,360
to beat the posted line.

43
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:22,810
Signal three is injury context, and it
goes beyond the player

44
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:23,840
named on the prop.

45
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,160
If a high-usage teammate is out, another
player may gain minutes,

46
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:29,880
touches, or shot attempts.

47
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:34,120
If that teammate returns, the added
opportunity may disappear.

48
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,810
An injury on the opposing team can also
change the matchup

49
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:39,640
or the likely game plan.

50
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:43,050
This is why injury news can create a chain
reaction across

51
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:46,780
an entire lineup. Check the player, key
teammates,

52
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:50,850
and relevant opponents. Then check again
close to game time,

53
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:55,300
because late status changes can make an
earlier analysis obsolete.

54
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:00,010
Signal four is the game environment: the
conditions that can raise

55
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:02,450
or lower opportunity for everyone
involved.

56
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:06,000
Depending on the sport, that may include
pace,

57
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:10,780
expected possessions, projected game
script, weather, venue,

58
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,420
rest, or the market’s expectation for
total scoring.

59
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:17,960
A faster basketball game can create more
possessions.

60
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:22,020
A football team playing from behind may
throw more often.

61
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,660
Wind can affect passing and kicking.

62
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:28,360
Rest and travel can influence minutes or
efficiency.

63
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:33,290
No single factor decides the prop, but the
environment tells you

64
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:36,220
whether tonight’s setting supports the
opportunity you expect.

65
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:38,680
Signal five is recent form.

66
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:42,150
It is useful, but it is also the easiest
signal to

67
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,570
misuse. A player going over in seven of
the last ten

68
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:49,720
games is context—not a seventy-percent
forecast for tonight.

69
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,410
Those games may have featured different
lines,

70
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:54,560
roles, opponents, or teammates.

71
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:57,880
Use recent performance to identify a
trend,

72
00:03:58,160 --> 00:03:59,820
then investigate what caused it.

73
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:03,460
Did minutes increase? Did usage change?

74
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:10,870
Was the player simply converting at

75
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:11,030
an unusually high rate?

76
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:12,440
Most importantly, compare each result with
tonight’s exact line.

77
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:16,870
Clearing twenty-two points in several
games does not answer a prop

78
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:18,500
set at twenty-four and a half.

79
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:21,180
Signal six is market pricing.

80
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,700
Sportsbooks build a margin into their
odds,

81
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:26,520
often called the vig.

82
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:30,290
Removing that margin gives you a cleaner
estimate of the probability

83
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:31,720
implied by the market.

84
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:35,990
That no-vig probability is not a promise
that the market is

85
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,560
right. It is a useful baseline.

86
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,790
Comparing the line and price across books
can also reveal whether

87
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:46,820
the market broadly agrees or whether
meaningful disagreement exists.

88
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:50,860
For pick’em platforms, where the payout
structure works differently,

89
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:55,470
the same principle still applies:
understand the line in the context

90
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:58,740
of the broader market instead of judging
it in isolation.

91
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:05,910
Now comes the

92
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:07,010
important part: do not treat the six
signals as six equal

93
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:08,460
votes. The strongest driver depends on the
sport and prop.

94
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:11,460
Minutes may dominate one basketball
analysis.

95
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:15,100
A pitcher matchup may matter more for a
baseball prop.

96
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:17,940
Late injury news can outweigh an older
trend.

97
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:22,650
Propeller’s sport-specific agents evaluate
the relevant data that is available

98
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:24,100
for the selected prop.

99
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:27,440
The agent breakdown shows where the
analysis agrees,

100
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,580
where it conflicts, and why.

101
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:34,000
The confidence score then summarizes the
strength of that directional signal.

102
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:37,750
Confidence is not a guarantee, and it
should not be read

103
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:40,540
as the player’s exact chance of hitting
the prop.

104
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:44,010
It is a research signal designed to help
you understand the

105
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:47,280
analysis more quickly. Here is the
complete workflow.

106
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:52,260
First, confirm the player, stat,
direction, and current line.

107
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:57,730
Second, evaluate volume, matchup,
injuries, environment, recent form,

108
00:05:57,740 --> 00:06:02,910
and market pricing. Third, identify the
main driver and any disagreement

109
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:08,210
between signals. Fourth, verify that the
line and news are still

110
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:12,310
current. Finally, decide whether the
evidence is strong enough to research

111
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,920
further—or whether the right move is to
skip the prop.

112
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,150
A consistent process matters more than
forcing an opinion on every

113
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:28,790
available line. That is how

